WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.630
A cult classic beloved by its legions of devoted
fans, Veronica Mars put a fresh spin on the

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:07.430
TV mystery genre.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:11.530
Former young adult novel author Rob Thomas
created the show, a modern-day combination

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:16.520
of noir and Nancy Drew, and it ran for three
seasons on UPN and the CW.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.240
Years later, the show would be resurrected
as a successfully crowdfunded movie.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.789
Let's explore the real reasons why Veronica
Mars was canceled.

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:25.230
Low ratings

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:29.289
Television networks are businesses, and low-rated
shows tend to be poor investments.

00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:33.500
“This serves as a preemptive apology for
the conversation that’s about to take place.”

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:39.059
In its first season back in the Fall of 2004,
Veronica Mars ranked a dismal #148 out of

00:00:39.059 --> 00:00:40.510
156 total shows.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:45.870
It ticked up a bit to #145 in its second season,
and #138 in its third and final year.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:50.399
All the while, total viewership held steady
at about 2.5 million viewers.

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:51.420
“Tada”

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.940
Said CW entertainment president Dawn Ostroff
at a TV industry event shortly after the cancellation:

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.010
"We really tried every single year to bring
in more viewers and we just weren't able to

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:01.010
crack it."

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:04.920
Maybe that's true, but the network didn't
do Veronica Mars any favors by scheduling

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.440
it against Fox's American Idol at the peak
of its popularity.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.130
“That is slightly disturbing.”

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.470
Tough fit with the CW

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:17.030
In 2006, the two struggling networks, the
WB and UPN, merged into a new network called

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.890
the CW, which became Veronica Mars' new home.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:21.720
“This face, right here.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.009
My over the moon face.”

00:01:24.009 --> 00:01:27.340
Sadly, despite surviving the merger, it wasn't
an easy fit.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:31.790
Even though CW corporate sibling Warner Bros.
Studios produced Veronica Mars, it was canceled

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:33.670
because it couldn't draw in new viewers.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:38.180
“So are you gonna tell me why I am here,
or should I just sit back and enjoy your impression

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.399
of a mildly constipated David Caruso.”

00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:45.689
That's because the CW was going after a couple
of very specific audience niches, and Veronica

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:49.600
Mars—a difficult-to-categorize show that
was a little funny and a little dramatic—didn't

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.880
fit in with the vampires and superheroes.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:53.159
Ahead of its time

00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:58.079
Veronica Mars ran on broadcast TV just before
the so-called "Second Golden Age of Television,"

00:01:58.079 --> 00:02:01.270
and before streaming services opened up new
opportunities for quirkier shows like the

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.180
kind Rob Thomas creates.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.649
Before shows like Breaking Bad and Game of
Thrones found accolades for their intricate

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:11.220
plotting that required viewers to pay close
attention and watch every episode, TV shows

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:15.110
that dared to be innovative, cinematic, and
complicated were often critically acclaimed

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:16.670
but commercially ignored.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:20.250
Veronica Mars aired around the same time as
Arrested Development, another show that pulled

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.580
in a small but devoted audience of fans who
appreciated precision.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:24.700
“Coo Coo cacha.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:25.920
Coo Coo cacha.”

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:27.980
“What are you doing?”

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.100
“Michael and women, a-coodle-doodle-do.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:33.220
“That is what I was just telling him.”

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:33.730
“ a-coodle-doodle-do.”

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:34.230
“Cha”

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:36.300
No reboot

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.020
While Veronica Mars was still on the air,
some CW executives asked Thomas to write a

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.520
show about rookie cops.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.610
He countered with the idea of making the fourth
season of Veronica Mars jump forward in time

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:49.900
to show Veronica in her first year as an FBI
agent, and they produced a pilot presentation:

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.400
a mini-episode about 12 minutes long.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.251
While well-received by CW brass, the reboot
wasn't meant to be—Thomas explained in an

00:02:57.251 --> 00:03:00.130
interview that he thinks CBS president Les
Moonves, killed it:

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:03.340
“We went from dead to back on the air to
dead again very quickly.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:07.230
Our Hail Mary pass came very close to working
but didn’t quite get past all the filters

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.340
it needed to.”

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:10.560
Moving on

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.550
Probably seeing the writing on the wall, Thomas
had already secured his next gig by the time

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.410
the series was wrapping up its third season.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.790
“Those days are over and it is time to move
on.”

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:23.010
He was hired on as a writer and producer for
ABC's Miss Guided, a sitcom starring Judy

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.340
Greer as a high school guidance counselor.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.120
Thomas was quickly promoted to showrunner
and executive producer, but left the series

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.900
after only a few weeks due to creative differences.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:35.720
Miss Guided was ultimately a midseason replacement
that aired only seven episodes in the spring

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:39.510
of 2008, freeing up Greer to move on to better
things.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:40.510
Resurrection

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:44.670
Veronica Mars's hardcore fans couldn't let
go, and neither could the show's talent . For

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:49.200
years after the cancellation, Rob Thomas and
star Kristen Bell mentioned the possibility

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.250
of a movie in interviews.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:54.130
It seemed like a longshot for a studio to
finance a big-screen version of a show very

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:55.130
few people watched.

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:57.050
“When are we going to do this whole Veronica
Mars movie, huh.”

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:00.670
“I don’t know, you got a couple million
dollars lying around?”

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.970
But then along came Kickstarter.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:06.920
In March 2013, Thomas launched a campaign
to finance the movie directly from fans.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.960
Within 30 days, the project had collected
an astonishing $5.7 million.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.920
“Wait for it… ohhhhh.”

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:17.880
“Yeah”

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:20.980
“I love all 91 thousand of you.”

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:24.620
“From the whole cast, thank you so very
much.”

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:28.310
Thomas took a screenplay and his Kickstarter
money to Warner Bros., and the studio kicked

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:30.390
in a few more million to get the movie made.

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:33.550
It was released to theaters and to video-on-demand
platforms in March 2014.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:34.550
Thanks for watching!

